FREAD(3)                       P32W Unix                        FREAD(3)

NAME
       fread, fwrite - buffered binary input and output

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdio.h>

       size_t fread(void *p, size_t size, size_t n, FILE *f);
       size_t fwrite(const void *p, size_t size, size_t n, FILE *f);

DESCRIPTION
       fread reads n items of size bytes each from the stream f into
       the memory at p, stopping early at the end of the input or on
       an error. fwrite writes n items of size bytes from p to f.

       Both go through the stream's buffer. fread copies what the
       buffer holds and refills it 512 bytes at a time; fwrite on a
       fully buffered stream copies whole runs into the buffer,
       while on a line-buffered or unbuffered stream (stdout on a
       terminal, stderr) it writes a character at a time, as
       fputc(3) does.

RETURN VALUE
       The number of whole items read or written: n if all went
       well, less (perhaps 0) at the end of the input or after an
       error; 0 if size is 0. Bytes of an incomplete last item are
       read (or written) but not counted. Use feof(3) and ferror(3)
       to tell why fread stopped.

EXAMPLES
       struct stat st;
       if (fread(&st, sizeof st, 1, f) != 1)
           ...                     /* short file or error */

       n = fread(buf, 1, sizeof buf, stdin);    /* bytes */

SEE ALSO
       read(2), write(2), stdio(3), getc(3), putc(3), ferror(3)

BUGS
       size * n is not checked for overflow. For large transfers
       read(2) and write(2) on the descriptor (fileno(3)) avoid the
       copy through the buffer; do not mix them with buffered calls
       on the same stream without fflush(3).
